  [1mWhat removing it actually does:[0m [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m ([38;5;153mserver.js:7[39m) is the only route on this server returning real data — [38;5;153m{
[39m  [38;5;153mquarter: "Q1", revenue: 42 }[39m. [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m is the only thing gating it; drop it and the endpoint is anonymous to
  anyone who can reach the process. "Internal" is a network claim, not an auth one: unless something in front of this
  (VPN, mesh, ingress ACL) is enforcing it, the route becomes open, and nothing in this repo enforces it. There's no
  other check downstream — [38;5;153mauth.js[39m is the whole story.

  Also worth noting: [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m only tests that a [38;5;153msession=[39m substring exists in the cookie header. It doesn't verify
  the signature, so the bar it sets today is already low — but low is not zero, and removing it removes the last thing
  that makes an unauthenticated request fail.

  [1mAlternatives that unblock the dashboard script without opening the route:[0m
  - Give the script a service credential — a static bearer token from env checked alongside the cookie path. A few
    lines, keeps anonymous requests out.
  - Serve the dashboard's data from a separate read-only route with its own auth, leaving [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m alone.
  - Have the script obtain a session cookie via [38;5;153m/login[39m the way a browser does.

  Which way do you want to go? If you've confirmed there's an enforced network boundary in front of this and you still
  want the check dropped, say so and I'll make the one-line change.

